p/new/ceshi/code_combo/edb79dae/8/code.py\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\n succeeded in 0ms:\n# Combo variant of edb79dae (index 8)\n# combo_spec: D5/3+D15/2\n# Name: 轮廓放置+XOR重叠\n\nimport random\nfrom typing import Iterable\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_coords, components_mask, get_bg, paste, random_free_location\n\n\n# Design analysis for combo D5/3 + D15/2\n# ---------------------------------------\n# a) Concrete behavior and flow trace\n#    The preserved scaffold is still the original one: one gray rectangular frame contains\n#    source-color placement cues, while the same colors also appear on external sprite templates\n#    and on compact two-color legends that define the final recoloring.\n#    The fused change is concentrated in the placement stage.\n#    - Original abstract rule: a same-color sprite is copied to each frame cue location and then\n#      recolored by the legend.\n#    - D5/3 alone: copy only the sprite's 4-neighbor outline; interior foreground pixels become\n#      background. Example with a 5x5 near-solid sprite: the original would keep the whole 5x5\n#      foreground shape, while D5 leaves a hollow shell.\n#    - D15/2 alone: if two placements claim the same cell, that cell should not show the later\n#      sprite; it should disappear to background.\n#    - Fused rule here: each cue is a single anchor pixel inside the frame, each odd-sized sprite\n#      outline is centered on that anchor, and overlapping outline cells cancel. Two neighboring\n#      anchors can therefore create a Venn-diagram-like result: exclusive contour arcs survive,\n#      contour intersections vanish.\n# b) Complexity calibration\n#    Keeping the original generator's non-overlapping NxN placeholder blocks would make D15/2\n#    latent, because equal-size placements could never overlap. To make the conflict rule real\n#    without introducing hidden numeric logic, the generator switches the frame cues to explicit\n#    single-pixel anchors on a dense horizontal lattice. Sprite sizes stay small and odd (3 or 5),\n#    so \"center on anchor\" is visually obvious and pairwise overlap can be produced deliberately.\n#    The number of source colors is capped at 2-3 and every source color is reused, so humans get\n#    repeated evidence for both the outline rule and the legend recoloring.\n# c) Hidden-assumption audit against the original solver\n#    - `_find_frame(...)` only assumes one rectangular border component. Still valid.\n#    - The old placeholder extractor assumed same-size solid blocks and used their bbox size to\n#      identify the matching external sprite. That assumption breaks completely once the cues are\n#      single pixels. The fused solver therefore extracts every non-bg interior pixel as one anchor\n#      and identifies sprites by \"same source color outside the frame but not part of any legend\".\n#    - The old writeback loop relied on top-left bbox alignment and overwrite semantics. Both break:\n#      placement is now center-based on an anchor pixel, and the compositor must count claims before\n#      deciding visibility.\n#    - Legend parsing via compact overlapping two-color glyphs is unchanged and still valid.\n# d) Placement legality\n#    The generator uses only odd sprite sizes and places anchors with a half-size margin from the\n#    frame border, so every centered outline fits fully inside the frame. Anchors are arranged in\n#    one or two rows with controlled horizontal stride, which creates horizontal pair overlaps while\n#    keeping maximum coverage at 2 and avoiding triple conflicts. External sprites and legends are\n#    still placed with a reserved blocker rectangle plus padding, so they never overlap or clip.\n# e) Visual observability\n#    A human sees three independent visible clues in 2-4 examples:\n#    - input anchors are single colored dots inside the frame;\n#    - output shapes are hollow contours, not solid copies of the external templates;\n#    - where neighboring contours intersect, cells disappear instead of showing one color on top.\n#    No invisible ordering by color id is used; colors only provide explicit same-color matching and\n#    legend-guided recoloring.\n# f) Visual cleanliness\n#    Clutter is controlled by restricting interaction to horizontal neighbors on the same row. Rows\n#    are vertically separated enough that there is no cross-row overlap, so the eye can segment each\n#    local XOR event cleanly. The scene still has the original hierarchy: outside objects explain the\n#    rule, inside the frame shows the result.\n# g) Change inferability\n#    The color mapping is still fully exposed by the external legends, so the new cognitive load is\n#    geometric rather than arbitrary. Because every source color is reused and at least one adjacent\n#    anchor pair uses different colors, the viewer can infer both \"same-color chooses shape\" and\n#    \"overlapping outlines erase\" from structure alone.\n# 9) New assumptions introduced by this combo\n#    - Every external sprite bbox must have odd height and width so a unique center anchor exists.\n#      The generator enforces `n in {3, 5}` for all sprites.\n#    - Every generated sprite must lose at least one interior cell under the hollowing rule, or D5\n#      would be visually inert. `_sprite_mask(...)` guarantees at least one removable interior cell.\n#    - The XOR compositor is defined as \"visible iff exactly one placement claims the cell\". The\n#      generator guarantees the stronger condition `max_claim_count <= 2`, so this matches pairwise\n#      XOR exactly and avoids ambiguous multi-color odd-parity cases.\n\n\nexec\n/bin/bash -lc 'ls -la /home/lixinyu/snap/new/solvers | head' in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\n succeeded in 0ms:\ntotal 12\ndrwxrwxrwx+  3 lixinyu lixinyu 4096 Apr 23 00:29 .\ndrwxrwxrwx+ 25 lixinyu lixinyu 4096 May 15 09:03 ..\ndrwxrwxrwx+  2 lixinyu lixinyu 4096 May  6 22:39 __pycache__\nlrwxrwxrwx   1 lixinyu lixinyu   34 Ap